articles.body:
The FIA announced Cost Cap breaches by Williams and Alpine for the 2025 F1 season on November 25, for which both teams have been fined.

Since the introduction of the Cost Cap in F1 by the FIA in 2021, Red Bull was the first team to be found irregular after a minor overspend. Even Aston Martin and Williams were found to have procedural breaches.

Aston Martin was once again found to have a procedural breach in 2024. And now, Williams and Alpine have been found to have breached the Cost Cap for the 2025 season after the FIA's report was published on November 25.

The Reporting Documentation states that nine F1 teams complied with the Cost Cap, leaving only the two teams named above. On the Power Unit Manufacturers' side, all five manufacturers were in compliance.

The FIA notes that Williams Grand Prix Engineering Limited acted in good faith throughout the process of review. There was no evidence that they acted in bad faith with the overspend, and they gained no advantage from it.

The FIA found an overspend of £627k, or 0.48%, which falls at the lower end of the <5% minor overspend range. Williams accepted the breach for the Full Year Reporting under:

exclusion of costs pursuant to Article 3.1(i) of the Financial Regulations (concerning costs of HR, Legal and Finance Activities);

adjustment of costs pursuant to Article 4.1(f)(iii) of the Financial Regulations (concerning Redundant Inventories).

As per the Accepted Breach Agreement, Williams accepts an undisclosed financial penalty and a 1.5% reduction in wind tunnel and CFD testing over the next 12 months. The FIA applies the cut by subtracting 1.5% from the coefficient that sets Williams' wind tunnel and CFD limits. The FIA said the breaches came from an interpretation of the Financial Regulations that Williams adopted in good faith.

On the other hand, Alpine also accepted a Procedural Breach for the 2025 Cost Cap. It is related to inaccuracies in reporting in Full Year Reporting, under:

exclusion of costs pursuant to Article 3.1(a) of the Financial Regulations (concerning costs in respect Marketing Activities);

exclusion of costs pursuant to Article 3.1(h) of the Financial Regulations (concerning costs of Non-F1 Activities);

exclusion of costs pursuant to Article 3.1(i) of the Financial Regulations (concerning costs of HR Activities);

exclusion of costs pursuant to Article 3.1(k) of the Financial Regulations (concerning costs of Employee's Bonus Costs);

adjustment of costs pursuant to Article 4.1(b) of the Financial Regulations (concerning offsetting of costs);

adjustment of costs pursuant to Article 4.1(f) of the Financial Regulations (concerning costs of Inventories).

It is not an overspend, as the FIA said Alpine's Relevant Costs for 2025 were below the Cost Cap. Due to the nature of the breach, Alpine will incur an undisclosed financial penalty and will cover the costs of the Cost Cap Administration. Both Williams and Alpine accepted their respective breaches.
